whyRefuted:
REFUTED — the quoted CSS is real, but the browser mechanism the finding rests on is false in both engines, including the one in the repro (iPad Safari).

1) The line is there. `sed -n '105,120p' src/ui/style.css | cat -A` shows `  touch-action:none;$` at line 113, inside the `html,body` block that starts at line 94. So the code exists as claimed. Everything after that does not hold.

2) The claim "'none' on body disables panning in every descendant scroller regardless of their own touch-action:auto" is wrong. Both engines stop the ancestor walk at the scroller and re-enable panning there. I downloaded and read the actual sources rather than reasoning from memory:

WebKit — Source/WebCore/style/StyleAdjuster.cpp, `computeUsedTouchAction`, lines 186-195 verbatim:
    // https://w3c.github.io/pointerevents/#determining-supported-touch-behavior
    // "A touch behavior is supported if it conforms to the touch-action property of each element between
    // the hit tested element and its nearest ancestor with the default touch behavior (including both the
    // hit tested element and the element with the default touch behavior)."
    bool hasDefaultTouchBehavior = isScrollableOverflow(style.overflowX()) || isScrollableOverflow(style.overflowY());
    if (hasDefaultTouchBehavior)
        usedTouchAction = ComputedStyle::initialTouchAction();
with `isScrollableOverflow` (line 180) = `overflow == Overflow::Scroll || overflow == Overflow::Auto`. The inherited value is DISCARDED and reset to `auto` at any element with `overflow:auto`. Called at line 765: `style.setUsedTouchAction(computeUsedTouchAction(style, m_parentStyle.usedTouchAction()))`.

Blink — third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/resolver/style_adjuster.cc, comment at line 1000 and the call at 1007:
    // The effective touch action is the intersection of the touch-action values
    // of the current element and all of its ancestors up to the one that
    // implements the gesture. Since panning is implemented by the scroller it is
    // re-enabled for scrolling elements.
    inherited_action = AdjustTouchActionForElement(inherited_action, builder, parent_style, element);
and `AdjustTouchActionForElement` (lines 119-153) ORs `kPanX`/`kPanY` back into the inherited action when `scrolls_overflow` is true. So the candidate's own cited `ComputedStyle::EffectiveTouchAction` is not a straight top-down intersection — the intersection input is patched at every scroller first.

3) Every panel the finding names is exactly the case that triggers the reset — `overflow:auto`, both axes:
  - src/ui/style.css:414  `#context.kiln{ display:block;overflow:auto; }`
  - src/ui/style.css:466  `.glaze-list{...overflow:auto;min-height:0;align-content:start}`
  - src/ui/style.css:619  `#ov-inner{... overflow:auto; ...}`
  - src/ui/style.css:991  `#diag{... max-height:calc(var(--vh) * 0.62); overflow:auto; ...}`
On the scroller itself the used/effective action becomes pan-x|pan-y, and its descendants inherit that, so a finger starting anywhere inside the kiln schedule pans it. The player drags the firing panel up and it moves.

4) Nothing else blocks it either. `grep -n "touch-action" src/ui/style.css` returns exactly two hits — 113 and 124 — and 124 is `#gl`, which is a SIBLING of `#hud` (index.html:16 vs 20), not an ancestor of any panel. `grep -rn "touchAction|touchmove|touchstart|preventDefault" src/ --include=*.js` finds no `touchmove` listener and no JS writing `style.touchAction`; the only document-level preventDefault is src/main.js:238-240, `for (const g of ['gesturestart','gesturechange','gestureend'])`, which is WebKit's two-finger pinch and does not touch single-finger panning.

5) The subsidiary claim "the body declaration buys nothing" is also wrong, and wrong for the same reason. `html,body` carry `overflow:hidden` (src/ui/style.css:95), so they are NOT scrollable overflow — they get no pan re-enable — which is precisely why `none` there still suppresses page-level pan/pinch for touches that land on the HUD rather than on `#gl`. Removing it would regress the thing its comment at lines 109-112 describes.

6) The measurements may well be accurate — the kiln panel probably does overflow at 834x1080 — but overflowing is the authored design, not the bug: src/ui/style.css:412-414 says "it is a curve and six controls that all have to be reachable, so it scrolls as a whole if it must", and it does scroll. A cut-off-content finding would need a different accusation and a different repro; this one is a false alarm on its stated mechanism.

Confidence: high. The only way this survives is a WebKit version predating `computeUsedTouchAction`'s `hasDefaultTouchBehavior` reset, and that reset has been in WebKit since touch-action shipped in Safari 13 — well below anything an iPad runs today.
